<div class="mb-8">
  <Heading size="2xl" as="h1" class="mb-2">Badge</Heading>
  <Text size="lg" tone="subdued" as="h2">
    A small visual indicator for status, labels, or counts. Badges draw attention to new or
    important information without interrupting the user's flow.
  </Text>
</div>

<!-- Canonical example -->
<div class="mb-8">
  <Example source={'
    <Badge>Badge</Badge>
  '}/>
</div>

<!-- Usage -->
<div class="mb-12">
  <Heading size="lg" as="h2" class="mb-4">Usage</Heading>
  <div class="border rounded-lg bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <CodeBlock code={"<Badge variant=\"default\">New</Badge>\n<Badge variant=\"secondary\">In Progress</Badge>\n<Badge variant=\"destructive\">Overdue</Badge>"}/>
  </div>
  <Prose class="mt-4">
    <p>
      Badges are inline elements that sit next to text, inside buttons, or alongside other components.
      Use the <code>variant</code> prop to communicate the semantic meaning of the badge.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</div>

<!-- When to Use -->
<div class="mb-12">
  <Heading size="lg" as="h2" class="mb-4">When to Use</Heading>
  <Prose>
    <p>
      Badges work best for small, glanceable metadata: status labels on list items, category tags,
      notification counts, or "new" indicators. They communicate state without taking up much space.
    </p>
    <p>
      Avoid badges for long text or full sentences — they are meant to be scanned, not read. If the
      information requires an explanation or action, consider an
      <a href="/components/alert">Alert</a> or inline message instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Badges are not buttons. If clicking should trigger an action, use a
      <a href="/components/button">Button</a> with a small size instead.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</div>

<!-- Behaviour -->
<div class="mb-12">
  <Heading size="lg" as="h2" class="mb-4">Behaviour</Heading>
  <Prose>
    <p>
      Badges are purely visual — they carry no interactive behaviour by default. They render as
      a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> with <code>inline-flex</code> layout, so they align naturally with
      surrounding text and icons.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pill shape (fully rounded corners) is intentional: it distinguishes badges from buttons
      and other rectangular UI elements at a glance.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</div>

<!-- Variants -->
<div class="flex flex-col gap-12 mb-12">
  <div>
    <Heading size="md" as="h3" class="mb-2">Variants</Heading>
    <Prose class="mb-4">
      <p>Five visual styles to match different contexts. Use <strong>default</strong> for primary emphasis,
        <strong>secondary</strong> for neutral metadata, <strong>destructive</strong> for errors or urgency,
        <strong>outline</strong> for subtle labels, and <strong>ghost</strong> for minimal presence.</p>
    </Prose>
    <Example source={'
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <Badge variant="default">Default</Badge>
        <Badge variant="secondary">Secondary</Badge>
        <Badge variant="destructive">Destructive</Badge>
        <Badge variant="ghost">Ghost</Badge>
        <Badge variant="outline">Outline</Badge>
      </div>
    '}/>
  </div>

  <div>
    <Heading size="md" as="h3" class="mb-2">With Icons</Heading>
    <Prose class="mb-4">
      <p>Combine badges with icons for richer status indicators. Add <code>gap-1.5</code> for spacing
        between icon and text. Small icons like notification dots can sit flush against the label.</p>
    </Prose>
    <Example source={'
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <Badge variant="default" class="gap-1.5"><Icon name="circle-check" size="sm"/> Completed</Badge>
        <Badge variant="secondary" class="gap-1.5"><Icon name="spinner" size="sm"/> Processing</Badge>
        <Badge variant="outline" class="pl-4">(5) New messages <Icon name="circle" size="xs" class="text-blue-500 ml-1"/></Badge>
      </div>
    '}/>
  </div>

  <div>
    <Heading size="md" as="h3" class="mb-2">Status Dots</Heading>
    <Prose class="mb-4">
      <p>Use the <code>dot</code> prop to render a small colored circle before the badge text.
        The dot color adapts to the badge variant — on filled badges it inherits the text color,
        on outline and secondary badges the tone color is visible directly.</p>
    </Prose>
    <Example source={'
      <Stack gap="4">
        <div>
          <Text size="xs" tone="subdued" as="div" class="mb-2">default (primary)</Text>
          <InlineStack gap="2" wrap>
            <Badge dot="default">Default</Badge>
            <Badge dot="success">Active</Badge>
            <Badge dot="warning">Pending</Badge>
            <Badge dot="danger">Failed</Badge>
            <Badge dot="info">Syncing</Badge>
            <Badge dot="subdued">Archived</Badge>
          </InlineStack>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Text size="xs" tone="subdued" as="div" class="mb-2">secondary</Text>
          <InlineStack gap="2" wrap>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="default">Default</Badge>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="success">Active</Badge>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="warning">Pending</Badge>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="danger">Failed</Badge>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="info">Syncing</Badge>
            <Badge variant="secondary" dot="subdued">Archived</Badge>
          </InlineStack>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Text size="xs" tone="subdued" as="div" class="mb-2">outline</Text>
          <InlineStack gap="2" wrap>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="default">Default</Badge>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="success">Active</Badge>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="warning">Pending</Badge>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="danger">Failed</Badge>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="info">Syncing</Badge>
            <Badge variant="outline" dot="subdued">Archived</Badge>
          </InlineStack>
        </div>
      </Stack>
    '}/>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Best Practices -->
<div class="mb-12">
  <Heading size="lg" as="h2" class="mb-4">Best Practices</Heading>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
    <div class="border rounded-lg p-4 border-green-500/30 bg-green-500/5">
      <Heading size="sm" as="h4" class="mb-2 text-green-600">Do</Heading>
      <Prose>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep labels to one or two words.</li>
          <li>Use consistent variants for the same status across the app.</li>
          <li>Pair with icons when the meaning isn't obvious from text alone.</li>
          <li>Use the outline variant for low-priority or informational tags.</li>
        </ul>
      </Prose>
    </div>
    <div class="border rounded-lg p-4 border-red-500/30 bg-red-500/5">
      <Heading size="sm" as="h4" class="mb-2 text-red-600">Don't</Heading>
      <Prose>
        <ul>
          <li>Use more than three or four badges in a row — it becomes visual noise.</li>
          <li>Mix too many variant colours in one view.</li>
          <li>Use destructive badges for non-critical information.</li>
          <li>Rely on colour alone to convey meaning — add text or an icon for accessibility.</li>
        </ul>
      </Prose>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- API Reference -->
<div>
  <Heading size="lg" as="h2" class="mb-4">API Reference</Heading>
  <div class="border rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-medium">Prop</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-medium">Type</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-medium">Values</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-medium">Default</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y">
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">variant</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2">Symbol</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">default, secondary, destructive, ghost, outline</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">dot</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2">Symbol</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">default, success, warning, danger, info, subdued</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs">nil</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>
